---
import PageHeader from '~/components/PageHeader.astro';
import Base from '~/layouts/Base.astro';
import { PROVISIONAL_BELOW, TIERS, TRACK_FULL_DAYS, WEIGHTS } from '~/lib/score';
import { SITE } from '~/lib/site';
import { TECHNIQUES, TECHNIQUE_META } from '~/lib/taxonomy';

const toc = [
  { id: 'principles', label: 'Principles' },
  { id: 'status', label: 'How status is derived' },
  { id: 'score', label: 'Security Score' },
  { id: 'evidence', label: 'Evidence rules' },
  { id: 'lab', label: 'The Lab' },
  { id: 'outputs', label: 'What we host' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'Known limitations' },
];
---

<Base title="Methodology" description="How luau.site derives break status, computes the Security Score, and decides what counts as evidence.">
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Project"
    title="Methodology"
    description="Every number on luau.site comes from code you can read and data you can audit. Here's exactly how it works."
  />
  <div class="container-page grid gap-12 py-12 lg:grid-cols-[13rem_minmax(0,1fr)]">
    <nav aria-label="On this page" class="hidden lg:block">
      <ul class="sticky top-24 space-y-2 text-sm">
        {toc.map((t) => <li><a href={`#${t.id}`} class="text-muted hover:text-fg">{t.label}</a></li>)}
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <article class="prose-site max-w-[44rem]">
      <h2 id="principles">Principles</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Computed, not typed.</strong> Status, time to break and score are pure functions of the dataset (<a href={`${SITE.repo}/blob/main/src/lib/status.ts`}><code>status.ts</code></a>, <a href={`${SITE.repo}/blob/main/src/lib/score.ts`}><code>score.ts</code></a>), covered by unit tests.</li>
        <li><strong>Sourced or it doesn't ship.</strong> Every entry and every claim links to evidence. CI rejects data without it.</li>
        <li><strong>Public history.</strong> The data lives as YAML in a public repository, so every change is a reviewable commit.</li>
        <li><strong>No paid placement.</strong> Vendors can't buy a position or a removal. They can submit corrections and statements like anyone else.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="status">How status is derived</h2>
      <p>Each obfuscator <em>version</em> has one of three statuses, derived from the deobfuscators that target it:</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Broken</strong></td><td>At least one tool has <em>full</em> support for this version and outputs readable (or near-original) source.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Partial</strong></td><td>Tools exist, but they only recover constants, bytecode or traces, or their support is partial or experimental.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Holding</strong></td><td>No public tool is tracked for this version. This means "none known", not "unbreakable".</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        The <strong>break date</strong> is the earliest date one of the breaking tools became public, either its first-seen date or a per-version
        <code>since</code> date. <strong>Time to break</strong> is the break date minus the release date. When either date is only month-precise or
        marked approximate (<code>~</code>), the duration is shown with a <code>~</code> and rounded to coarser units.
      </p>
      <p>
        A maintainer can override a derived status in rare cases, for example a break confirmed privately. An override must include a written note and
        sources, and it is labelled on the page.
      </p>

      <h2 id="score">Security Score</h2>
      <p>The score runs from 0 to 100 and has four components, all evaluated against the obfuscator's <em>current</em> version:</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Weight</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Resistance</strong></td><td>{WEIGHTS.resistance}</td>
            <td>Holding 50 · broken only by private or paid tools 30 · partial 20 · broken by a free closed tool 5 · broken by an open-source tool 0. When several tools break it, the most widely available one counts.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Track record</strong></td><td>{WEIGHTS.track}</td>
            <td>Median <em>survival</em> across dated versions: time to break, or time since release if still unbroken. Full points at {TRACK_FULL_DAYS} days or more.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Lab results</strong></td><td>{WEIGHTS.lab}</td>
            <td>The share of our benchmark samples (for the current version) that <em>no</em> deobfuscator recovered with matching behavior.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Technique depth</strong></td><td>{WEIGHTS.technique}</td>
            <td>A capped checklist of documented techniques. VM virtualization counts most. Compression and watermarking count zero.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <h3>Missing data and provisional scores</h3>
      <p>
        If a component has no data (no dated versions, no Lab runs), it gets <strong>neutral half credit</strong>. We don't drop it and re-scale the
        rest, because missing evidence shouldn't push a score up or down. The share of the weight that is backed by real data is shown as{' '}
        <em>data %</em>. Below {Math.round(PROVISIONAL_BELOW * 100)}%, the score is <strong>provisional</strong> and its tier is drawn with a dashed border.
      </p>
      <h3>Tiers</h3>
      <p>{TIERS.map((t) => `${t.tier} ≥ ${t.min}`).join(' · ')}.</p>
      <h3>Technique points</h3>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Points</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{TECHNIQUES.map((t) => <tr><td><a href={`/learn#${t}`}>{TECHNIQUE_META[t].label}</a></td><td>{TECHNIQUE_META[t].points}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      </table>

      <h2 id="evidence">Evidence rules</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Every obfuscator, deobfuscator, auth service and hand-written event needs at least one source with an access date. Archive links are encouraged.</li>
        <li>Primary sources (repositories, official docs, vendor changelogs) beat screenshots and second-hand reports.</li>
        <li>Entries carry a <code>verified</code> flag and a <code>lastVerified</code> date. After {SITE.staleAfterDays} days without re-verification, an entry is flagged as possibly outdated.</li>
        <li>A deobfuscator only counts once it's publicly verifiable: a repository, a public release, or a reproducible demonstration. Rumors don't count.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="lab">The Lab</h2>
      <p>
        The Lab is our own benchmark. We write small test scripts, obfuscate each one with every obfuscator, run every deobfuscator on the result, and
        then execute all three stages with the official <code>luau</code> CLI. A deobfuscation counts as <em>recovered</em> only if its output prints
        exactly what the original printed. See <a href="/lab">the Lab</a> for the corpus and how to contribute runs.
      </p>

      <h2 id="outputs">What we host, and what we don't</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>We host our own corpus scripts and the outputs produced from them.</li>
        <li>We <strong>don't</strong> host third-party scripts, their deobfuscated output, or deobfuscator binaries. We link to the public source.</li>
        <li>Nothing here is meant to help anyone bypass licensing on a specific script. The goal is transparency about how strong protection actually is.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="limits">Known limitations</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>"Holding" reflects what we know. Private tools that stay private are invisible to us.</li>
        <li>Release dates for many versions aren't publicly documented, so track record often rests on approximate dates.</li>
        <li>Lab coverage depends on maintainers running paid obfuscators by hand, so it grows over time.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
